{
  "opening": "This release introduces several new utilities and unifies existing caching mechanisms to improve consistency and functionality. It also addresses specific edge cases in argument handling and error propagation to ensure robust behavior across various use cases.",
  "requirements": [
    {
      "id": "m01",
      "text": "The public API must expose the `has_path` function, which returns a boolean indicating whether a specified path exists within a nested collection. Additionally, `get_in`, `update_in`, and `zip_dicts` must remain available as part of this change's scope."
    },
    {
      "id": "m05",
      "text": "The release must provide the `throttle` decorator for controlling the rate at which a function can be called. Supporting names such as `decorator`, `limit_error_rate`, `process_beat`, and `wrapper` must also be accessible."
    },
    {
      "id": "m07",
      "text": "Both `memoize` and `cache` decorators must support raising `skip` exceptions to bypass caching logic. They must also expose `.memory`, `.invalidate`, and `.invalidate_all` attributes with identical behavior."
    },
    {
      "id": "m08",
      "text": "The `reraise` decorator must accept a callable for its `into` argument to dynamically determine the resulting exception type. The related `api_call` and `fallback` functions must continue to operate correctly within this context."
    },
    {
      "id": "m13",
      "text": "Decorators created via `@decorator` must now correctly handle optional arguments when the underlying function uses keyword-only arguments. Public names including `decorator_fab`, `func`, `get_argnames`, `has_1pos_and_kwonly`, `has_single_arg`, and `rate_limit` must reflect this capability."
    },
    {
      "id": "m14",
      "text": "The `autocurry` mechanism must correctly handle keyword arguments during partial application. The functions `get_spec`, `rcurry`, and `rpartial` must exhibit this corrected kwargs handling behavior."
    },
    {
      "id": "m16",
      "text": "The `throttle` decorator must correctly process `timedelta` arguments for rate limiting configurations. This fix ensures that `limit_error_rate` behaves accurately when time durations are specified."
    }
  ],
  "closing": "Please ensure all public names listed above are exported and behave as described. These changes aim to enhance the library's reliability and feature set for Python 3.9 users."
}
